tgs:Monologue.Frankly_106_TenThingsWorthMoreThanGold a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "10 Things Worth More Than a Pound of Gold"@en ;
    skos:definition "Prompted directly by gold hitting a real, documented all-time high the week of recording, Nate works through his own real, structured countdown of things he argues are worth more than a pound of gold — moving deliberately from purely financial comparisons (cash, silver, Bitcoin) toward increasingly intangible ones (freedom, sanity, integrity, the web of life, and finally life itself)."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/106-10-things-worth-more-than-a-pound-of-gold> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6wUntJ1OTmw> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Frankly-106-Transcript-10-Things-Worth-More-Than-a-Pound-of-Gold.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.TenThingsWorthMoreThanGold, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly walks through the real, financial half of his list first: $50,000 in cash outvalues a pound of gold at real, then-current prices, but he's direct that money itself ultimately reduces to trust in a system backed by energy surplus and civilizational complexity, not an intrinsic store of value; Bitcoin similarly outvalues gold today while depending entirely on continuous electricity and connectivity, making its real resilience genuinely untested against gold's own multi-millennial track record. A real, personal story anchors his case for integrity directly: recalling being corrected by his own father as a teenager for telling a lie, he credits that real, specific conversation with shaping a lifelong understanding that integrity, unlike money, cannot be traded, borrowed, or faked for long. He's real and direct that the web of life itself isn't external to the economy but constitutes it, arguing humanity currently operates inside what he calls a 'consensus trance' where record stock and gold prices coexist with six of nine planetary boundaries already breached and a seventh, ocean acidification, close behind. Closes on real, direct personal reflection: at roughly 80 years of likely lifespan, every human is 'really paying with the wages of our lives' for whatever they acquire, framing life itself as gold's only genuine rival — 'even with life, gold is just a shiny rock.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely institutional/statistical (gold price history, planetary boundaries, mental health prevalence data) rather than pointing to further individuals — Lyn Alden is directly name-checked ('there's no stopping this train'), already built elsewhere in this graph, not a new find."@en ;
    .


